WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.821
In this last part of the lecture

00:00:02.821 --> 00:00:05.801
we hand arrays and objects to methods.

00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:08.551
A method receives an int, an array

00:00:08.551 --> 00:00:09.620
or an object.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.676
Can it change the value the caller has?

00:00:12.753 --> 00:00:15.579
And can it make the caller's variable

00:00:15.656 --> 00:00:17.260
point somewhere else?

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.083
The answers follow from one rule

00:00:20.083 --> 00:00:22.330
and once you see the machine

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:23.754
frames and arrows

00:00:23.829 --> 00:00:25.777
every example in this part

00:00:25.852 --> 00:00:27.650
becomes easy to predict.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.231
There are two ways a language

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.749
can pass an argument.

00:00:31.749 --> 00:00:33.129
With pass by value

00:00:33.129 --> 00:00:35.889
the value is copied into the parameter

00:00:35.889 --> 00:00:37.614
which is a new variable

00:00:37.683 --> 00:00:39.685
so assigning to the parameter

00:00:39.754 --> 00:00:41.617
does not affect the caller.

00:00:41.617 --> 00:00:43.273
With pass by reference

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:45.688
the parameter is just another name

00:00:45.757 --> 00:00:47.551
for the caller's variable

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.966
as with an int & parameter

00:00:50.035 --> 00:00:51.070
in C++.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.647
Java always passes by value.

00:00:53.647 --> 00:00:56.113
But for an array or an object

00:00:56.193 --> 00:00:58.738
the value stored in the variable

00:00:58.818 --> 00:01:01.920
is a reference, an arrow to the object.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.500
Passing it copies the arrow, not the object.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.277
So a method can change the object

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.556
through its copy of the arrow

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.130
but it can never make the caller's

00:01:13.204 --> 00:01:15.410
variable point somewhere else.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.550
Start with an int.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:21.017
main's frame on the call stack holds i

00:01:21.017 --> 00:01:22.317
equal to 5.

00:01:22.317 --> 00:01:26.043
The call, change(i), pushes a new frame

00:01:26.043 --> 00:01:28.210
with its own variable i

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.070
initialised with a copy of 5.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:32.607
Inside change

00:01:32.607 --> 00:01:35.320
i = 10 changes that copy

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.015
and only that copy.

00:01:37.015 --> 00:01:40.576
When change returns, its frame is popped

00:01:40.576 --> 00:01:42.780
and the 10 goes with it.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:46.158
So the program prints i = 5

00:01:46.158 --> 00:01:48.850
both before and after the call.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:50.561
Now an object.

00:01:50.561 --> 00:01:53.791
In main, s refers to a new StringBuffer

00:01:53.872 --> 00:01:55.003
holding "Hello".

00:01:55.003 --> 00:01:57.426
The variable holds the arrow

00:01:57.426 --> 00:01:59.849
the object lives on the heap.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:03.160
Calling change(s) pushes a frame whose

00:02:03.241 --> 00:02:06.310
parameter t gets a copy of that arrow.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.071
Two arrows, one object.

00:02:09.176 --> 00:02:12.635
So t.append("world !") changes the

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:15.780
very object that s refers to.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:19.303
When change returns, t disappears

00:02:19.303 --> 00:02:22.663
but the change to the object stays.

00:02:22.663 --> 00:02:26.490
After the call, main prints "Hello world".

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:30.299
A tempting idea: inside change

00:02:30.299 --> 00:02:33.112
give t a brand-new object

00:02:33.112 --> 00:02:35.404
and main will see it.

00:02:35.404 --> 00:02:36.758
Let's check.

00:02:36.862 --> 00:02:39.571
Again t starts with a copy

00:02:39.675 --> 00:02:41.550
of the arrow in s.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.176
The first line

00:02:43.176 --> 00:02:45.729
t = new StringBuffer("Hi")

00:02:45.729 --> 00:02:47.941
points t at a new object.

00:02:47.941 --> 00:02:49.047
From now on

00:02:49.047 --> 00:02:52.110
t and s point to different objects.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.258
The append changes only the new one

00:02:55.258 --> 00:02:57.300
which becomes "Hi world".

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:00.123
Main's object was never touched.

00:03:00.123 --> 00:03:03.370
When change returns, its frame is popped

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:06.539
and nothing refers to "Hi world" any more

00:03:06.539 --> 00:03:07.930
so it is garbage.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.324
So both lines print "Hello".

00:03:10.402 --> 00:03:13.154
Assigning to a parameter only moves

00:03:13.232 --> 00:03:15.040
the method's own arrow.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:16.556
Your turn.

00:03:16.556 --> 00:03:19.083
An array is an object too.

00:03:19.083 --> 00:03:23.200
Main makes x, the array {7, 8, 9}.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.475
zeroFirst sets a[0] to 0.

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:28.721
replace sets a to a new

00:03:28.815 --> 00:03:30.780
array of three zeros.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.246
Main calls zeroFirst(x)

00:03:34.246 --> 00:03:36.439
then replace(x).

00:03:36.439 --> 00:03:38.978
What is x afterwards?

00:03:38.978 --> 00:03:42.210
Pause the video and decide.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:48.854
zeroFirst gets a copy of the arrow in x.

00:03:48.854 --> 00:03:51.993
Through it, a[0] = 0 changes

00:03:52.068 --> 00:03:53.338
the array itself.

00:03:53.338 --> 00:03:54.908
The frame is popped

00:03:54.908 --> 00:03:57.150
but the array keeps its 0.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.339
replace also gets a copy of the arrow.

00:04:00.413 --> 00:04:03.775
a = new int[] makes the method's own

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:06.688
arrow point to a fresh array of zeros.

00:04:06.688 --> 00:04:08.331
When replace returns

00:04:08.331 --> 00:04:11.020
that arrow and that array are gone.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.604
So x is {0, 8, 9}.

00:04:13.604 --> 00:04:16.440
The first method is like append

00:04:16.526 --> 00:04:19.533
the second is like the reassignment

00:04:19.619 --> 00:04:20.650
we just saw.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:25.182
Here is a job: swap the first and the last

00:04:25.276 --> 00:04:27.158
element of an array.

00:04:27.158 --> 00:04:31.391
A natural first try passes the two elements

00:04:31.486 --> 00:04:33.744
swapWrong of array[0]

00:04:33.838 --> 00:04:35.908
and array[n - 1].

00:04:36.002 --> 00:04:38.166
Its frame gets a =

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.330
1 and b = 5

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.870
copies of the two numbers.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:47.494
tmp = a, a = b, b = tmp.

00:04:47.494 --> 00:04:51.038
Inside the method the swap works

00:04:51.038 --> 00:04:53.540
a is 5 and b is 1.

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:55.486
But those were copies.

00:04:55.486 --> 00:04:57.010
The frame is popped

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.912
and the array is still 1, 2, 3

00:04:59.912 --> 00:05:00.783
4, 5.

00:05:00.783 --> 00:05:03.250
Passing the elements cannot work.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:06.286
The right way passes the array

00:05:06.286 --> 00:05:08.900
which means a copy of the reference

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:10.722
and the two positions

00:05:10.722 --> 00:05:13.416
swapElements of the array, 0

00:05:13.416 --> 00:05:16.030
and the last index, n - 1.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.453
tmp = a[p1] saves the 1.

00:05:19.536 --> 00:05:22.443
a[p1] = a[p2] copies

00:05:22.526 --> 00:05:24.353
the 5 to the front

00:05:24.436 --> 00:05:26.595
and a[p2] = tmp

00:05:26.678 --> 00:05:28.671
puts the 1 at the end.

00:05:28.754 --> 00:05:30.748
These assignments change

00:05:30.831 --> 00:05:32.741
the cells of the array

00:05:32.741 --> 00:05:34.900
through the copied reference.

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:40.760
After the call, main sees 5, 2, 3

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.170
4, 1.

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.827
Now a loop that swaps element i with

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:48.306
element n - i - 1

00:05:48.306 --> 00:05:51.126
while i is less than n / 2.

00:05:51.126 --> 00:05:54.032
i = 0 swaps the two ends

00:05:54.117 --> 00:05:56.852
which puts the 1 back in front

00:05:56.938 --> 00:05:58.989
and the 5 at the end.

00:05:58.989 --> 00:06:02.750
i = 1 swaps positions 1 and 3

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:04.630
the 2 and the 4.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:08.563
The result is 1, 4, 3, 2, 5

00:06:08.563 --> 00:06:10.952
the array we had, reversed.

00:06:10.952 --> 00:06:13.512
The loop stops at n / 2.

00:06:13.597 --> 00:06:16.413
Running it all the way to n would

00:06:16.498 --> 00:06:18.375
swap every pair twice

00:06:18.375 --> 00:06:21.020
and leave the array as it was.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.290
The same question with objects.

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:28.194
Main has two points, obj1 at 1, 2

00:06:28.194 --> 00:06:30.883
and obj2 at 10, 20

00:06:30.883 --> 00:06:34.787
and calls swap(obj1, obj2).

00:06:34.874 --> 00:06:37.824
The parameters objA and objB

00:06:37.911 --> 00:06:40.600
are copies of the two arrows.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.155
tmp = objA

00:06:43.155 --> 00:06:46.305
tmp also points to 1, 2.

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:49.035
objA = objB

00:06:49.035 --> 00:06:52.815
objA now points to 10, 20.

00:06:52.815 --> 00:06:55.125
objB = tmp

00:06:55.125 --> 00:06:58.590
objB now points to 1, 2.

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:01.125
Inside swap, the swap worked

00:07:01.125 --> 00:07:03.385
and the prints there show it.

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:06.323
But the method never assigned anything

00:07:06.399 --> 00:07:08.508
to obj1 or obj2.

00:07:08.584 --> 00:07:10.467
After the call they point

00:07:10.542 --> 00:07:12.200
where they always did

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:14.460
obj1 is still 1, 2.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:16.810
To swap the contents

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:19.422
change the objects themselves

00:07:19.422 --> 00:07:21.107
through the arrows.

00:07:21.192 --> 00:07:24.478
swapValues saves the x and y of objA

00:07:24.562 --> 00:07:26.669
in temporary variables,

00:07:26.753 --> 00:07:29.113
copies the x and y of objB

00:07:29.197 --> 00:07:30.545
into objA,

00:07:30.545 --> 00:07:34.000
and puts the saved values into objB.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:37.522
Now the objects themselves have changed

00:07:37.522 --> 00:07:40.694
so main sees obj1 at 10, 20

00:07:40.694 --> 00:07:42.914
and obj2 at 1, 2.

00:07:42.994 --> 00:07:45.531
Java has no statement that swaps

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:47.831
two variables of the caller;

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.368
you write the three assignments

00:07:50.448 --> 00:07:52.430
where the variables live.

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:56.445
The same rule covers plain assignment.

00:07:56.445 --> 00:08:00.150
Declaring b = a copies the arrow

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:03.766
so a and b are two names for one array

00:08:03.766 --> 00:08:04.579
aliases.

00:08:04.579 --> 00:08:08.647
To get a second, independent array, copy it

00:08:08.647 --> 00:08:11.810
for example by calling clone on a.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.487
Now b[0] = 99 changes

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:18.315
the one array that a and b share.

00:08:18.315 --> 00:08:21.974
a and b both print 99, 2, 3

00:08:21.974 --> 00:08:25.218
while c, the clone, is still 1, 2

00:08:25.218 --> 00:08:25.800
3.

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:29.618
Likewise, == on arrays asks

00:08:29.618 --> 00:08:31.575
is it the same array?

00:08:31.575 --> 00:08:34.332
d holds the same numbers as a

00:08:34.332 --> 00:08:36.911
but it is a different array

00:08:36.911 --> 00:08:39.668
so a == d is false

00:08:39.668 --> 00:08:41.891
while Arrays.equals

00:08:41.891 --> 00:08:45.270
which compares the contents, is true.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:47.571
Let's step back and look

00:08:47.643 --> 00:08:49.149
at the whole lecture.

00:08:49.221 --> 00:08:51.158
An array stores elements of

00:08:51.229 --> 00:08:52.807
one type side by side

00:08:52.807 --> 00:08:54.816
and we reach them by index.

00:08:54.816 --> 00:08:56.035
Given the index

00:08:56.035 --> 00:08:58.402
an element is found in one step

00:08:58.402 --> 00:09:00.195
random access, O(1).

00:09:00.267 --> 00:09:02.418
A sorted array can be searched

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.929
with binary search, in O(log n).

00:09:04.929 --> 00:09:06.650
And arrays are compact

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:09.160
and sit underneath stacks, queues

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:10.810
heaps and hash tables.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:13.914
The price: the capacity is fixed when

00:09:13.988 --> 00:09:15.551
the array is created

00:09:15.551 --> 00:09:18.603
so growing means a new array and a copy

00:09:18.603 --> 00:09:19.198
O(n).

00:09:19.273 --> 00:09:22.175
And inserting or deleting in the middle

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:25.078
means shifting all the later elements

00:09:25.078 --> 00:09:26.120
O(n) again.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:28.274
To sum up this part.

00:09:28.274 --> 00:09:31.579
One: Java passes every argument by value

00:09:31.579 --> 00:09:34.018
and for an array or an object

00:09:34.018 --> 00:09:36.142
that value is a reference.

00:09:36.142 --> 00:09:39.290
Two: through its copy of the reference

00:09:39.368 --> 00:09:41.729
a method can change the object

00:09:41.808 --> 00:09:43.460
or the array's cells.

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:44.310
Three

00:09:44.354 --> 00:09:47.308
assigning to a parameter never changes

00:09:47.386 --> 00:09:49.096
the caller's variable.

00:09:49.174 --> 00:09:52.127
That is why swapWrong and swap of two

00:09:52.205 --> 00:09:54.770
points do nothing for the caller.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:56.811
Try this yourself.

00:09:56.811 --> 00:10:00.943
Move all the zeros of 1, 0, 2, 0

00:10:00.943 --> 00:10:03.760
0, 3, 4 to the end

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:07.611
keeping the order of the other elements

00:10:07.611 --> 00:10:09.301
in a single pass.

00:10:09.301 --> 00:10:12.400
Hint: keep a write index for the

00:10:12.494 --> 00:10:14.560
next non-zero element.

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:18.413
Next lecture, we put arrays to work

00:10:18.413 --> 00:10:21.430
insertion sort and merge sort.

